Subject: init: drop arguments for init past the limit instead of panicking

Samsung's bootloader appends its own parameters to the end of the boot
image's command line, so on a command line that hands init its arguments
after "--", every parameter it adds lands after the dashes too: on the
Galaxy S23+ that is more than CONFIG_INIT_ENV_ARG_LIMIT of them, and the
kernel panics in start_kernel() with "Too many boot init vars" before any
console but an early one is up.

The arguments the boot image gives come first, so keep the first
MAX_INIT_ARGS, report the rest once, and boot.

--- a/init/main.c
+++ b/init/main.c
@@ -487,9 +487,9 @@ static int __init set_init_arg(char *param, char *val,
 
 	for (i = 0; argv_init[i]; i++) {
 		if (i == MAX_INIT_ARGS) {
-			panic_later = "init";
-			panic_param = param;
+			pr_warn_once("Too many boot init vars, dropping `%s' and after\n",
+				     param);
 			return 0;
 		}
 	}
 	argv_init[i] = param;
